# =============================================================================
# THE VIDEO METHOD, APPLIED: a C/H around a NACA 0012 WITH A GLAZE-ICE DOUBLE
# HORN. 128 blocks, 0 inverted cells.
#
# Built from docs/research/nesting-blocks-at-a-complicated-leading-edge.md,
# which records GridPro's two videos: when a leading edge becomes geometrically
# complicated you add blocks LOCALLY around the feature. The section it cites
# is not obtainable (that note's section 5), so naca0012_iced.dat beside this
# file is a shape of MINE, built by make_iced_body.py. Nothing about it is
# claimed to match GridPro's.
#
# WHAT IT TOOK, and all four are in section 29 with the measurement that forced
# them -- none was a guess:
#   --far-monotone   the far-field map's normal cast REVERSES across the ice
#                    valley, so the generator refuses. Arc length is monotone
#                    and folds the CLEAN section too (1786 of 14320), so it is
#                    not the answer either. This keeps the cast and repairs only
#                    the stations that reverse, by isotonic regression. It is
#                    INERT on the clean section -- verified byte-identical.
#   --pin-tips 1     a station law lands a block corner on the horn tip only by
#                    luck. Pinning one takes the fold count 8 -> 1.
#   --wall-band 0.02 the first layer must be thinner than the concave radius
#                    behind the horn, or it folds there: 0.05 and 0.03 leave 1
#                    inverted cell at (0.0487, 0.0988), 0.02 leaves none, and
#                    0.015 leaves 187.
#   --split 24       MORE blocks at the feature makes it WORSE here, which is
#                    the opposite of the method's premise: 32 -> 48 -> 64 goes
#                    8 -> 25 -> 99 inverted. Section 29e says why.
#
# QUALITY IS POOR AND IS NOT HIDDEN: max 76.3 deg, mean 10.3, against 50.1 and
# 1.8 for the same topology on the clean section. This is a mesh, not a good
# mesh.
#
#   python3 docs/research/gridpro-blocking-method/make_iced_body.py
#   python3 docs/research/gridpro-blocking-method/make_ch.py \
#       --body docs/research/gridpro-blocking-method/naca0012_iced.dat \
#       --layers 2 --split 24 --turn-blend 0.8 --wake 8 \
#       --far-monotone --pin-tips 1 --wall-band 0.02 --out gridpro_ch_iced.json
#   ./run.sh -conf docs/research/gridpro-blocking-method/gridpro_ch_iced.dat
#
# A RESEARCH HARNESS, not a shipped case.
#
MESH_MODE 1
MESH_TOPOLOGY_FILE docs/research/gridpro-blocking-method/gridpro_ch_iced.json

# The aerofoil the C-grid case already ships: two segments, upper and lower,
# split at the leading edge because that is where a block corner sits. There is
# NO far-field geometry -- the rectangle is declared, corner by corner.
GEOM_FILE docs/research/gridpro-blocking-method/naca0012_iced.dat

BL_INITIAL_THICKNESS 0.001
MB_SPLIT_QUADS 1

# The FALLBACK, and here it is load bearing: the far-field and outlet edges
# carry no binding, so this is where their condition comes from. The aerofoil's
# sides are bound and take 'wall' off their own sidecar. A real case would want
# inlet / outlet / farfield told apart, which needs a far-field geometry file.
BC_GEOM farfield

# The repo default of 20 sweeps DIVERGES on this family (section 8g); five is
# where the wall height is held and nothing has turned.
MB_SMOOTH_ITERS 5

EXPORT_VTK 1
OUTPUT_FILENAME results/meshes/gridpro_ch_iced/mesh_gridpro_ch_iced.vtk
